<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=flintlock">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flintlock</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1610</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as true flintlock</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A flintlock is a firearm that fires by striking a flint to make a spark, and the name also covers the mechanism itself. The French court gunsmith Marin le Bourgeoys made a firearm with the mechanism known as the true flintlock for Louis XIII soon after his accession in 1610, combining features of earlier flint locks such as the snaplock and snaphance. It was in use across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by 1630 and was the standard European infantry weapon in the late 17th century. Flintlocks reloaded about twice as fast as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a> and misfired far less often, and percussion caps replaced them in the early to mid 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flintlock.jpg" alt="Flintlock" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Musee-historique-lausanne-img_0086.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Durrani army was built on cavalry armed with <b>flintlocks</b>, which made hit-and-run attacks combining new firearms with Turco-Mongol tactics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The British military had <b>flintlocks</b>, caplocks and breech-loading rifled artillery, while the Qing had matchlock muskets with far fewer rounds and lower accuracy.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought with European <b>flintlock</b> muskets, steel swords and cutlasses, were drilled constantly and fired on command, and by the late 19th century the kingdom had a large arsenal of breech-loading rifles.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dahomey">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Dahomey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1610-1615</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Marin le Bourgeoys · <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Firearms with some form of flint ignition were in use for over half a century before: the snaplock was probably invented shortly before 1517 and was certainly in use by 1547.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musket<span>by 1521</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=flintlock">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
